---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { url } from '../lib/paths';
import DeckMockup from '../components/DeckMockup.astro';

const articles = (await getCollection('articles')).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.pubDate.valueOf() - b.data.pubDate.valueOf()
);

// Unsplash photo "Lost in the Wilderness" by Geranimo (@geraninmo), served as
// a compressed WebP. Largest above-the-fold element, so loaded eagerly (LCP).
const heroImgBase = 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1487525219605-eadb39ae229c?crop=entropy&cs=tinysrgb&fit=crop&fm=webp&q=70';
const heroImg = `${heroImgBase}&w=1600&h=900`;
const heroSrcSet = [900, 1280, 1600, 2000]
  .map((w) => `${heroImgBase}&w=${w}&h=${Math.round((w * 9) / 16)} ${w}w`)
  .join(', ');

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Best OutDoor Wears',
    url: 'https://bestoutdoorwears.com',
    description: 'Outdoor survival and safety skills for beginners, taught as a card game.',
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Outdoor Survival Skills for Beginners: The Complete Guide | Best OutDoor Wears"
  description="Outdoor survival skills for beginners — navigation, water, shelter, fire, weather and emergencies, explained plainly. Plus Survival Cards, an 89-card deck that teaches them as a game."
  canonicalPath="/"
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <section class="hero-banner">
    <img
      class="hero-bg"
      src={heroImg}
      srcset={heroSrcSet}
      sizes="100vw"
      width="1600"
      height="900"
      loading="eager"
      fetchpriority="high"
      decoding="async"
      alt="Mist drifting through a dense evergreen forest at dawn"
    />
    <div class="hero-banner-inner">
      <span class="label">Outdoor Survival Skills for Beginners</span>
      <h1>Learn this <span class="accent">before</span> you need it.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Almost nobody gets into trouble outdoors because they lacked expensive kit. They get into trouble
        because of a decision made hours earlier, calmly, with plenty of options still available. This guide
        covers the skills that prevent that — and the deck that makes them stick.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={url('/product/')}>Get Survival Cards — $14</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#skills">Start with the skills ↓</a>
      </div>
      <p class="free-link">
        <a href={url('/downloads/survival-cards-free-sample.pdf')}>Free download: 8 cards from the deck (no email required) →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="hero-credit">Photo: Geranimo / Unsplash</p>
  </section>

  <section class="problem" id="problem">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>The three that<br>actually catch people</h2>
        <p class="desc">Not bears. Not cliffs. These, over and over.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="problem-grid">
      <div class="problem-card">
        <div class="num">01 / LATE</div>
        <h3>The turnaround that didn't happen</h3>
        <p>Everyone agrees on a turnaround time. Almost nobody honours it. Most serious incidents include a decision to push on past the last easy exit.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="problem-card">
        <div class="num">02 / COLD</div>
        <h3>Wet, then windy, then confused</h3>
        <p>Hypothermia doesn't need snow. Damp clothes, a breeze and a mild day are enough, and the first thing it takes is your judgement.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="problem-card">
        <div class="num">03 / ALONE</div>
        <h3>Nobody knew the plan</h3>
        <p>A search that starts with a known route and a known return time is short. One that starts with "he said something about the mountains" is not.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="skills">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>The order that<br>keeps you alive</h2>
        <p class="desc">When something goes wrong, this is the sequence. It almost never changes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="layers">
        <div class="layer-card layer-1">
          <span class="layer-index">FIRST</span>
          <h3>Stop</h3>
          <div class="swatch"></div>
          <p>Sit down. Drink something. The urge to keep moving is what turns being briefly unsure into being genuinely lost.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-card layer-2">
          <span class="layer-index">SECOND</span>
          <h3>Shelter</h3>
          <div class="swatch"></div>
          <p>Out of the wind, off the ground, dry if you can manage it. Exposure works far faster than thirst or hunger.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-card layer-3">
          <span class="layer-index">THIRD</span>
          <h3>Signal</h3>
          <div class="swatch"></div>
          <p>Be findable. Bright, geometric, noisy, in threes. Then keep doing it on a schedule instead of all at once.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:44px; max-width:74ch;">
        <h2>Why survival advice usually fails beginners</h2>
        <p>
          Search "survival skills" and you get two kinds of answer. The first is a wall of text about friction
          fire and building debris huts — genuinely interesting, almost never the thing that would have helped.
          The second is a kit list, because kit is easy to sell. Neither addresses what actually goes wrong,
          which is a chain of small, reasonable-seeming decisions made hours before anything felt like an
          emergency.
        </p>
        <p>
          The useful version is much less dramatic. It's knowing that you can survive a long time without food
          and a short time wet and cold, so you spend your effort accordingly. It's telling one person where
          you're going. It's turning back. None of it makes a good video, and all of it is what the people who
          come home do differently.
        </p>

        <h3>Water: the one you can't wait out</h3>
        <p>
          You can go days without eating. You cannot go days without drinking, and dehydration takes your
          decision-making long before it takes your legs. Carry more than you think, know where you'd refill,
          and treat everything you find — boiling is the method that works everywhere, without equipment or
          batteries. Filtering through cloth clears the grit but does nothing to the germs; the boil is the
          part that makes it safe.
        </p>

        <h3>Shelter: the ground takes more than the air</h3>
        <p>
          Most people building an emergency shelter start with the roof. It's the wrong order. Lying on cold
          ground pulls heat out of you faster than cold air does, so anything dry and thick underneath —
          leaves, bracken, a pack, a coiled rope — is worth more than a perfect roof. Get out of the wind,
          get off the ground, and stay small. A shelter you can fill with your own body heat beats a
          impressive one you can't.
        </p>

        <h3>Fire: preparation, not technique</h3>
        <p>
          Almost every failed fire is a tinder failure, not a spark failure. Build the whole thing before you
          strike anything: a nest of the finest dry material you can find, then progressively thicker kindling
          stacked and within reach. A ferro rod works soaked and costs very little. Friction fire is a genuine
          skill that takes most people many attempts in ideal conditions — worth learning, never worth
          depending on.
        </p>

        <h3>Navigation: hold a direction, don't chase a point</h3>
        <p>
          A shadow stick or an analogue watch gives you a rough heading, and rough is usually enough — the goal
          is to stop walking in circles, not to navigate precisely. But the more important habit happens before
          you leave: download the offline map, screenshot the route, and note the nearest road and the last
          junction you passed. A screenshot survives a dead data connection. A browser tab does not.
        </p>

        <h3>Being found: stay put and get big</h3>
        <p>
          If someone knows your route and expects you back, staying put and making yourself visible is almost
          always the right call. Wandering is what turns a short search into a long one. Spread bright kit,
          make straight lines and geometric shapes — nature makes very few of those, so they read as human from
          the air — and signal in threes on a schedule so you don't exhaust yourself or your batteries.
        </p>

        <h3>Food is the last thing to worry about</h3>
        <p>
          This is the one that surprises people. Hunger feels urgent and rarely is. Foraging burns energy,
          takes time you should be spending on being found, and carries real risk: deadly and edible plants and
          mushrooms look alike, several fatal species taste fine, and the old "universal edibility test" is not
          recommended by modern instructors because some toxins act in tiny amounts or long after the test
          would have passed. If you take one rule from this page: don't eat wild mushrooms, ever, on a
          field ID.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="product-section" id="product-teaser">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Survival Cards:<br>89 of these, in your pack</h2>
        <p class="desc">Everything above, one card at a time — plus the arguments that make it stick.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="product-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="product-includes">
            <div class="item"><span class="n">01</span><div><h4>52 core cards</h4><p>Quick hacks, smart shortcuts, survival skills, emergency response, multi-use kit.</p></div></div>
            <div class="item"><span class="n">02</span><div><h4>37 expansion cards</h4><p>Safety &amp; planning, camp cooking, a seven-day challenge, and ten scenario cards.</p></div></div>
            <div class="item"><span class="n">03</span><div><h4>Five ways to play</h4><p>Round a fire, in the car, or as a week of daily practice before you go.</p></div></div>
            <div class="item"><span class="n">04</span><div><h4>Print at home</h4><p>Nine to a page with cut guides, colour-coded so you find one fast.</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="price-tag">
            <span class="price">$14</span>
            <span class="was">one-time · instant download</span>
          </div>
          <div class="hero-actions" style="margin-top:20px;">
            <a class="btn btn-primary" href={url('/product/')}>See what's inside →</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <DeckMockup width={220} />
          <div class="prose" style="max-width:52ch;">
            <p>
              Reading about this stuff doesn't make it stick. Doing it once in your garden does, and arguing
              about it with four other people around a fire does even better. That's the whole reason this is
              a deck of cards and not another manual.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="articles">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Guides &amp; deep dives</h2>
        <p class="desc">Every skill on this page, in full.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="article-grid">
        {articles.map((a) => (
          <a class="article-card" href={url(`/articles/${a.slug}/`)}>
            <span class="label">{a.data.keyword}</span>
            <h3>{a.data.title}</h3>
            <p>{a.data.description}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="faq">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:74ch;">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Quick answers</h2>
      </div>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Is this a survival course?</summary>
        <p>No. It's a learning aid and a game. The emergency cards are memory prompts for people who've done a
          first aid course, not a replacement for one — and we say so on the cards themselves.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Does it teach foraging?</summary>
        <p>Deliberately not. Deadly and edible species look alike and field tests don't reliably work. The deck
          teaches the correct priority instead: you can go a long time without food.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Is it a physical deck?</summary>
        <p>It's a print-at-home PDF — nine cards a page with cut guides. Nothing ships, and you can reprint any
          card you lose.</p>
      </details>
      <p style="margin-top:20px;"><a href={url('/faq/')}>See the full FAQ →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
